  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html#textFieldSelection\r\n\r\n> Otherwise:​ if selection start is greater than start, then set it to start\\. \\(This snaps the start of the selection to the start of the new text if it was in the middle of the text that it replaced\\.\\)\r\n\r\nThere seems to be a bug in this definition.\r\n\r\nConsider the following:\r\n\r\nelement.value = \"123456789\";\r\nelement.setSelectionRange(4, 4);\r\nelement.setRangeText(\"abcd\", 3, 4);\r\n\r\nI would expect that the selection should be preserved (length zero) starting directly after the inserted text: 1234[]56789 -> 123abcd[]56789\r\n\r\nBut the definition in the standard says that it should be 1234[]56789 -> 123[a]bcd56789. But it contradicts the statement in parentheses: \"the selection ... was in the middle of the text that it replaced\" is clearly FALSE.\r\n",
